1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,480
A pH probe reads this white vinegar at

2
00:00:02,640 --> 00:00:04,280
two point four six.

3
00:00:04,560 --> 00:00:06,800
Dilute it ten times, and it reads two

4
00:00:06,880 --> 00:00:08,240
point nine six.

5
00:00:08,560 --> 00:00:12,240
Same vinegar, two different pH values, and neither

6
00:00:12,320 --> 00:00:13,920
one measures how much acid is in the

7
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:14,640
bottle.

8
00:00:14,960 --> 00:00:17,280
So how do you analyse a household sample

9
00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:19,760
so that every number you report means what

10
00:00:19,840 --> 00:00:20,960
you say it means?

11
00:00:22,967 --> 00:00:24,566
Start with the questions.

12
00:00:24,807 --> 00:00:26,966
How acidic is the vinegar right now?

13
00:00:27,367 --> 00:00:30,166
That's its pH, from a calibrated probe.

14
00:00:30,407 --> 00:00:32,606
How much acid does it hold in total?

15
00:00:32,807 --> 00:00:35,206
That's an amount, so it needs a titration.

16
00:00:35,607 --> 00:00:38,566
White vinegar is clear and reacts quickly, so

17
00:00:38,607 --> 00:00:41,446
a direct titration with phenolphthalein fits.

18
00:00:43,467 --> 00:00:45,066
Measure the pH first.

19
00:00:45,627 --> 00:00:48,106
Calibrate the probe with a pH seven buffer

20
00:00:48,347 --> 00:00:51,626
and, because vinegar reads below four, a pH

21
00:00:51,707 --> 00:00:54,906
two buffer, so its reading falls between them.

22
00:00:54,987 --> 00:00:57,386
A pH value describes the solution the probe

23
00:00:57,467 --> 00:00:59,866
is sitting in, so read the vinegar straight

24
00:00:59,947 --> 00:01:02,186
from the bottle, undiluted.

25
00:01:02,307 --> 00:01:04,986
Three readings agree, so report two point four

26
00:01:05,147 --> 00:01:05,706
six.

27
00:01:07,734 --> 00:01:08,933
Now the amount.

28
00:01:09,174 --> 00:01:12,373
The label says four percent acidity: about four

29
00:01:12,454 --> 00:01:15,813
grams of ethanoic acid in every hundred millilitres,

30
00:01:15,894 --> 00:01:18,533
so about zero point six seven moles per

31
00:01:18,614 --> 00:01:19,333
litre.

32
00:01:19,654 --> 00:01:22,693
The school's sodium hydroxide is about zero point

33
00:01:22,774 --> 00:01:24,173
one moles per litre.

34
00:01:24,454 --> 00:01:27,173
A twenty five millilitre aliquot of neat vinegar

35
00:01:27,334 --> 00:01:29,733
would need about a hundred and seventy millilitres

36
00:01:29,814 --> 00:01:32,373
of it, more than three burette fills.

37
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:36,800
You could use a stronger base, but half

38
00:01:36,880 --> 00:01:39,840
molar sodium hydroxide counts as corrosive in school

39
00:01:39,920 --> 00:01:40,800
safety guides.

40
00:01:41,280 --> 00:01:44,280
Instead, dilute the vinegar ten times.

41
00:01:44,560 --> 00:01:47,120
Pipette twenty five millilitres into a two hundred

42
00:01:47,240 --> 00:01:50,320
and fifty millilitre volumetric flask, make it up

43
00:01:50,360 --> 00:01:51,240
to the mark.

44
00:01:51,520 --> 00:01:54,080
The acid's moles don't change, they're just spread

45
00:01:54,160 --> 00:01:56,320
out, so you'll multiply by ten at the

46
00:01:56,400 --> 00:01:56,720
end.

47
00:01:57,280 --> 00:02:00,560
Now each titre should be about seventeen millilitres.

48
00:02:02,567 --> 00:02:05,846
The sodium hydroxide was standardised, because it absorbs

49
00:02:06,007 --> 00:02:09,126
carbon dioxide from the air: zero point zero

50
00:02:09,367 --> 00:02:12,166
nine nine four zero moles per litre.

51
00:02:12,487 --> 00:02:15,446
Pipette twenty five millilitre aliquots of the diluted

52
00:02:15,527 --> 00:02:18,406
vinegar and add two drops of phenolphthalein.

53
00:02:18,967 --> 00:02:21,526
Stop at the first faint pink that lasts

54
00:02:21,687 --> 00:02:22,726
thirty seconds.

55
00:02:24,734 --> 00:02:27,133
The rough run went to about seventeen point

56
00:02:27,214 --> 00:02:28,013
four.

57
00:02:28,094 --> 00:02:31,293
The accurate titres were seventeen point zero five,

58
00:02:31,374 --> 00:02:34,253
seventeen point one zero and seventeen point one

59
00:02:34,414 --> 00:02:37,773
five, all within zero point one zero, so

60
00:02:37,814 --> 00:02:41,053
the mean is seventeen point one zero millilitres.

61
00:02:43,067 --> 00:02:45,946
Ethanoic acid reacts one to one with sodium

62
00:02:46,027 --> 00:02:46,826
hydroxide.

63
00:02:47,307 --> 00:02:50,026
So the moles of hydroxide, one point six

64
00:02:50,107 --> 00:02:52,106
nine nine seven times ten to the minus

65
00:02:52,187 --> 00:02:54,626
three, equal the moles of acid in the

66
00:02:54,667 --> 00:02:55,546
aliquot.

67
00:02:55,867 --> 00:02:58,586
Divide by zero point zero two five zero

68
00:02:58,667 --> 00:03:02,186
zero litres: zero point zero six seven nine

69
00:03:02,307 --> 00:03:05,026
nine moles per litre in the diluted vinegar.

70
00:03:05,307 --> 00:03:08,026
Times ten for the dilution: the vinegar is

71
00:03:08,107 --> 00:03:10,586
zero point six seven nine nine moles per

72
00:03:10,667 --> 00:03:11,226
litre.

73
00:03:13,234 --> 00:03:16,033
The label's four percent means about four grams

74
00:03:16,114 --> 00:03:18,673
per hundred millilitres, so convert.

75
00:03:19,234 --> 00:03:21,553
Zero point six seven nine nine moles per

76
00:03:21,634 --> 00:03:24,673
litre, times sixty point zero five grams per

77
00:03:24,834 --> 00:03:27,713
mole, is forty point eight three grams per

78
00:03:27,794 --> 00:03:28,673
litre.

79
00:03:28,754 --> 00:03:30,993
That's four point zero eight three grams per

80
00:03:31,074 --> 00:03:34,513
hundred millilitres, just over the label's four grams,

81
00:03:34,594 --> 00:03:35,713
so it's close.

82
00:03:37,734 --> 00:03:40,933
The pH says how acidic the vinegar is.

83
00:03:40,974 --> 00:03:43,813
The grams per hundred millilitres say how much

84
00:03:43,894 --> 00:03:45,053
acid it holds.

85
00:03:45,254 --> 00:03:48,053
And they fit together: with K a, zero

86
00:03:48,214 --> 00:03:50,533
point six eight moles per litre of ethanoic

87
00:03:50,614 --> 00:03:53,253
acid predicts a pH of about two point

88
00:03:53,334 --> 00:03:54,213
four six.

89
00:03:54,454 --> 00:03:55,933
Why not run that backwards?

90
00:03:56,134 --> 00:03:58,613
A reading just zero point zero one out

91
00:03:58,854 --> 00:04:01,373
shifts the answer by about five percent.

92
00:04:01,654 --> 00:04:04,373
The titration counts every acid that reacts with

93
00:04:04,574 --> 00:04:08,213
hydroxide, so report it as titratable acidity, as

94
00:04:08,294 --> 00:04:09,413
ethanoic acid.

95
00:04:11,434 --> 00:04:12,873
Then report the limits.

96
00:04:13,154 --> 00:04:15,673
This result describes the tested bottle.

97
00:04:16,154 --> 00:04:18,473
More bottles must be sampled to describe the

98
00:04:18,554 --> 00:04:19,833
batch or brand.

99
00:04:20,074 --> 00:04:21,713
A few other acids could add to the

100
00:04:21,754 --> 00:04:22,233
titre.

101
00:04:22,634 --> 00:04:24,873
A percentage by mass would also need the

102
00:04:24,954 --> 00:04:26,313
vinegar's density.

103
00:04:26,434 --> 00:04:29,433
And pH changes with temperature, so record the

104
00:04:29,514 --> 00:04:30,233
temperature.

105
00:04:32,234 --> 00:04:36,313
Validity: the titration measures the total acid, because

106
00:04:36,394 --> 00:04:39,113
vinegar is clear, it reacts one to one

107
00:04:39,274 --> 00:04:41,913
and the endpoint is close to equivalence.

108
00:04:41,954 --> 00:04:43,753
And the pH was measured on the sample

109
00:04:43,874 --> 00:04:44,833
itself.

110
00:04:45,274 --> 00:04:49,433
Reliability: three concordant titres, and three pH readings

111
00:04:49,514 --> 00:04:50,633
that agree.

112
00:04:51,274 --> 00:04:55,353
Accuracy: standardised base, volumetric glassware, and a probe

113
00:04:55,514 --> 00:04:58,073
calibrated with buffers either side of the sample.

114
00:05:00,100 --> 00:05:01,500
The risk assessment.

115
00:05:01,780 --> 00:05:04,980
This sodium hydroxide sits just under the irritant

116
00:05:05,140 --> 00:05:07,780
limit, so treat a splash as harmful and

117
00:05:07,900 --> 00:05:09,060
wear safety glasses.

118
00:05:09,700 --> 00:05:13,460
Vinegar this dilute isn't classified, but never taste

119
00:05:13,540 --> 00:05:14,620
lab samples.

120
00:05:14,900 --> 00:05:18,260
Phenolphthalein solution is usually made with ethanol, so

121
00:05:18,340 --> 00:05:21,780
it's flammable, and phenolphthalein itself is classified as

122
00:05:21,860 --> 00:05:24,260
a substance that may cause cancer.

123
00:05:24,340 --> 00:05:26,500
So use two drops, and keep it capped

124
00:05:26,580 --> 00:05:28,180
and away from flames.

125
00:05:28,260 --> 00:05:29,380
Use a pipette filler.

126
00:05:31,400 --> 00:05:32,040
Your turn.

127
00:05:32,600 --> 00:05:35,720
A cleaning vinegar is labelled six percent acidity.

128
00:05:36,200 --> 00:05:38,920
A student plans to titrate twenty five millilitre

129
00:05:39,080 --> 00:05:42,440
aliquots of it, undiluted, with zero point zero

130
00:05:42,680 --> 00:05:45,400
nine nine four zero moles per litre sodium

131
00:05:45,480 --> 00:05:48,520
hydroxide from a fifty millilitre burette.

132
00:05:48,840 --> 00:05:51,240
Use the label to estimate the titre, and

133
00:05:51,320 --> 00:05:53,520
explain how the plan should change.

134
00:05:53,800 --> 00:05:55,080
Pause here to answer.

135
00:06:06,300 --> 00:06:09,180
Six grams per hundred millilitres is about one

136
00:06:09,340 --> 00:06:10,860
mole per litre.

137
00:06:10,940 --> 00:06:13,900
Twenty five millilitres holds about zero point zero

138
00:06:14,060 --> 00:06:16,620
two five moles, so it needs about two

139
00:06:16,700 --> 00:06:19,820
hundred and fifty millilitres of base, five burette

140
00:06:19,900 --> 00:06:20,620
fills.

141
00:06:20,940 --> 00:06:23,420
Dilute it ten times first, or pipette a

142
00:06:23,460 --> 00:06:25,580
tenth as much, and the titre drops to

143
00:06:25,660 --> 00:06:27,420
about twenty five.

144
00:06:27,540 --> 00:06:30,940
Refilling five times isn't the fix: every refill

145
00:06:31,140 --> 00:06:33,100
adds two more readings to get wrong.

146
00:06:35,100 --> 00:06:37,660
One more, this time a base.

147
00:06:37,980 --> 00:06:41,300
A student analyses a household ammonia cleaner.

148
00:06:41,580 --> 00:06:43,740
She pours some into an open beaker and

149
00:06:43,820 --> 00:06:46,300
leaves it on the bench for forty minutes.

150
00:06:46,460 --> 00:06:49,020
Then she dilutes a sample and titrates it

151
00:06:49,100 --> 00:06:51,340
with standardised hydrochloric acid.

152
00:06:51,660 --> 00:06:54,300
Explain the effect on her calculated concentration of

153
00:06:54,380 --> 00:06:55,940
ammonia in the cleaner.

154
00:06:56,220 --> 00:06:57,660
Pause here to answer.

155
00:07:08,867 --> 00:07:11,666
Ammonia is a gas dissolved in water, and

156
00:07:11,747 --> 00:07:14,146
some of it escapes from an open beaker.

157
00:07:14,467 --> 00:07:17,106
So her sample holds fewer moles of ammonia

158
00:07:17,187 --> 00:07:18,226
than the bottle does.

159
00:07:18,867 --> 00:07:21,826
Fewer moles need less acid, so the titre

160
00:07:21,907 --> 00:07:24,546
is smaller, and her result is too low

161
00:07:24,627 --> 00:07:26,546
for the cleaner in the bottle.

162
00:07:26,587 --> 00:07:30,146
Saying water evaporates, so it's too high, misses

163
00:07:30,227 --> 00:07:32,546
that ammonia leaves much faster.

164
00:07:34,567 --> 00:07:38,086
So, to analyse a household sample: ask whether

165
00:07:38,127 --> 00:07:41,126
you need its pH, an amount, or both.

166
00:07:41,447 --> 00:07:44,326
Measure pH on the sample as it is.

167
00:07:44,487 --> 00:07:46,726
Plan the titre from the label, dilute if

168
00:07:46,767 --> 00:07:49,366
you need to, then scale back up.

169
00:07:49,607 --> 00:07:52,006
Report each result in its own units, with

170
00:07:52,087 --> 00:07:53,166
its limits.

171
